          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Miguel de Cervantes</strong><br>\nMiguel de Cervantes was born in Alcalá de Henares, Spain, in 1547. He was the fourth child of a poor surgeon who moved his family around Spain in search of work. Cervantes's early education was erratic, as he switched schools with each move. Despite this, he developed a love for reading and storytelling, which shaped his future as a writer.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Service</strong><br>\nIn his early twenties, Cervantes enlisted in the Spanish Navy Marines and fought in the Battle of Lepanto in 1571. During the battle, he sustained a serious injury to his left hand, rendering it useless, which earned him the nickname \"El Manco de Lepanto.\" Despite his injury, he continued military service for several years.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Captivity in Algiers</strong><br>\nCervantes was captured by Barbary pirates in 1575 on his journey back to Spain after military service. He spent five years as a slave in Algiers before his family could raise enough money to pay his ransom. His experiences in captivity would later influence his writing, providing firsthand accounts of the complexities of human nature under duress.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
